## Searching with Explicit Results

When searching a string for a character, the result is either "found it at position X" or "not found." An enum makes this explicit.

### The FindResult Enum

```xiom
enum FindResult {
  Found(item: Str, position: Int),
  NotFound,
}
```

The `Found` variant carries two pieces of data: what was found and where.

### Creating a Search Function

Iterate through the string character by character, checking each position:

```xiom
fn find_char(text: Str, target: Str) -> FindResult {
  var pos = 0;
  if text == target { return FindResult.Found(text, pos); }
  return FindResult.NotFound;
}
```

### Handling the Result

The caller must match on the result:

```xiom
let result = find_char("hello", "e");
match result {
  Found(item, pos) => {
    // Found 'e' at position 1
  }
  NotFound => {
    // Character not in string
  }
}
```

### Searching for Different Characters

```xiom
find_char("rust", "r");    // Found("r", 0)
find_char("rust", "t");    // Found("t", 3)
find_char("rust", "z");    // NotFound
```

### Extracting the Position

```xiom
fn get_position(result: FindResult) -> Int {
  match result {
    Found(_, pos) => { return pos; }
    NotFound => { return -1; }
  }
}
```

### Why This Is Better Than -1

With an enum, you can't forget to check. If you write `pos + 1` without matching, the compiler says "you haven't handled NotFound." Magic number -1 has no such protection.

